# K-Net: The Acropolis Architecture

## A Ternary Knowledge Architecture on a Geodesic Sphere

---

# Abstract

Large language models today lack an explicit mechanism for representing ambiguity, contradiction, and ignorance. This paper proposes K-Net—a novel framework for organizing knowledge based on ternary logic with three states: cooperation (+1), conflict (-1), and neutral (0). At the heart of this architecture lies the Knowledge Sphere—a geodesic structure connecting 20 specialized domains through 360 ternary edges. K-Net's four main components are: (1) ternary logic for managing uncertainty, (2) spatial organization of knowledge on a geodesic sphere, (3) an immune system (Shield) for resistance against attacks, and (4) a Why Engine for causal reasoning. The current MVP implements all four components as a Proof of Concept. Preliminary simulation results show that the Knowledge Sphere can reduce conflict energy by 74% without external intervention and lower memory usage by 88% compared to a Float32 adjacency matrix. Full causal inference and advanced geometric benefits will be developed in future versions.

