# 2. Theoretical Foundations & Background

## 2.1 Basic Concepts

**Ternary Logic:** K-Net is built on a ternary logic whose roots go back to the work of Łukasiewicz (1920). Each state carries a distinct meaning:

| Symbol | Meaning             | Tangible Example            |
| :----- | :------------------ | :-------------------------- |
| +1     | Cooperation, agreement, truth | Friendship or alliance     |
| -1     | Conflict, opposition, falsehood | Rivalry or debate          |
| 0      | Neutral, uncertain, unknown   | A stranger with no defined relationship |

**Geodesic Sphere:** A geometric structure composed of 20 hexagons and 12 pentagons that follows Euler's polyhedron formula ($V - E + F = 2$). Each hexagon represents a specialized domain (mathematics, physics, chemistry, biology, philosophy, etc.).

## 2.2 Related Work

- **Ternlang / TIS Stack:** A ternary programming language (Smith et al., 2025).
- **GNRFET Ternary Multiplier:** Ternary hardware using graphene nanoribbons (Chen & Park, 2024).
- **ReTern:** Fault tolerance in ternary LLMs (Brown et al., 2025).
- **Switch Transformers (MoE):** Mixture‑of‑experts architecture (Fedus et al., 2022).

## 2.3 K-Net Positioning

| Feature                | MoE                  | MoR                  | K-Net                                      |
| :--------------------- | :------------------- | :------------------- | :----------------------------------------- |
| Structure              | Flat                 | Recursive            | **Hierarchical & geometric**               |
| Uncertainty            | None (hallucination) | None (hallucination) | **State 0 (“I don't know”)**               |
| Knowledge organization | Implicit             | Implicit             | **Explicit (physical location on sphere)** |
| Optimization           | Gradient‑based       | Gradient‑based       | **Gradient‑free (Simulated Annealing)**    |

