## 3.2 Core Components

### 3.2.1 Knowledge Sphere

The Knowledge Sphere is a two‑layer geodesic structure. The macro layer consists of 20 hexagons and 12 pentagons ($V=60, E=90, F=32$). Each hexagon is subdivided into 6 spherical triangles—totaling 180 spherical triangles. Counting shared edges, the number of unique edges is 360.

**Clarification on edge count:** 20 domains yield at most 190 unique relationships ($20 \times 19 / 2 = 190$). The current MVP uses 360 simulated edges to model local interactions on the sphere's surface. These edges form a multi‑edge graph in which a single pair of domains may have more than one edge—each edge representing a different aspect of the relationship.

### 3.2.2 Immune System (Shield)

K-Net Shield is a bio‑inspired security layer. The current MVP implements **Shield Lite**—a centralized entry gate that checks inputs with three ternary tests (removal, substitution, contradiction). Advanced versions (Shield Full and Shield Adaptive) are on the research roadmap (see papers 6 and 7).

### 3.2.3 Why Engine

The Why Engine is a conceptual 5‑step framework for causal reasoning: (1) removal test, (2) substitution test, (3) repeatability test, (4) ternary labeling (+1, 0, -1), and (5) deep analysis. The current MVP contains the skeleton of this cycle—the first three tests are implemented as placeholder functions that return sample results. Automatic causal inference and deep analysis (mechanism → principle → fundamental law) will be developed in future versions.

